# 22. WI-FI SECURITY EVOLUTION

Historically important security mechanisms:

```text
WEP
 ↓
WPA
 ↓
WPA2
 ↓
WPA3
```

Security improved significantly across these generations.

---

# 23. WEP

**WEP = Wired Equivalent Privacy**

WEP is an obsolete wireless security mechanism.

Major problems include:

- Weak cryptographic design
- Small IV space
- Key recovery attacks
- Poor security by modern standards

### Exam Point

> **WEP should not be used for modern secure Wi-Fi deployments.**

---

# 24. WPA

**WPA = Wi-Fi Protected Access**

WPA was introduced as an improvement over WEP.

It commonly used:

```text
TKIP
```

However, WPA/TKIP is now legacy technology and should not be selected for modern secure deployments.

---

# 25. WPA2

**WPA2** significantly improved Wi-Fi security.

It is commonly associated with:

```text
AES
CCMP
```

Two major deployment models:

### WPA2-Personal

Uses a shared password/PSK-based model.

```text
User
 ↓
Wi-Fi Password
 ↓
WPA2-Personal
```

### WPA2-Enterprise

Uses enterprise authentication infrastructure, commonly based on:

```text
802.1X
+
EAP
+
Authentication Server
```

---

# 26. WPA3

**WPA3** is the newer Wi-Fi security generation.

Important improvements include:

- Stronger password-based authentication design for Personal mode
- Improved protection against offline password guessing
- Enterprise security improvements
- Better security defaults

WPA3-Personal uses:

> **SAE – Simultaneous Authentication of Equals**

---

# 27. WPA3-PERSONAL

Instead of the older WPA2-PSK approach, WPA3-Personal uses **SAE**.

Conceptually:

```text
Client                    AP
  │                       │
  │──── SAE exchange ─────│
  │                       │
  │── Secure key setup ───│
  │                       │
  └──── Secure Wi-Fi ─────┘
```

### Important

SAE improves resistance to certain offline password-guessing attacks compared with traditional WPA2-PSK authentication.

---

# 28. WPA2 VS WPA3

| Feature | WPA2-Personal | WPA3-Personal |
|---|---|---|
| Authentication | PSK-based | SAE |
| Security | Strong when configured properly | Improved |
| Offline password attack resistance | Weaker | Improved |
| Modern recommendation | Still widely deployed | Preferred where supported |

---

# 29. WPA2-ENTERPRISE

Enterprise Wi-Fi can use:

```text
802.1X
+
EAP
+
RADIUS/authentication server
```

Architecture:

```text
Wireless Client
      │
      ▼
     AP
      │
      ▼
802.1X / EAP
      │
      ▼
